    },
    "crystal_structure": "Crystalline nanofibrils; hydrogen-bonded chain packing makes chitin very difficult to dissolve in water. Two polymorphs are distinguished: alpha-chitin (antiparallel chain packing, orthorhombic unit cell) is the dominant, most crystalline form (crustacean shells, insect cuticle); beta-chitin (parallel chain packing, monoclinic unit cell) occurs in squid pens and some diatoms.",
    "chain_flexibility_notes": "In composite with calcium carbonate (as in crustacean shells), forms much harder/stiffer structures than pure chitin.",
